## Which lld flavor reaches msil-ld (ImplementationPlan.md 5.9.1, M13; recon
## p2/lld-flavor.md 1.3). msil-ld is the msil flavor of lld: the copy named
## msil-ld, 'lld -flavor msil' (-flavor only as the first argument), and any
## program name with a '-'-separated component equal to 'msil', in any case,
## reach it. That rule comes before the first-component rule, so a
## triple-prefixed name with a 'gnu' component before 'msil'
## (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil-ld, x86_64-w64-windows-gnu-msil-ld) is
## msil-ld and not the GNU flavor, as it was before M13. Only whole components
## count: 'msilx-ld' is still the GNU flavor (from its 'ld' component), and
## 'ld.lld' and 'x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-ld' stay GNU. Plain 'lld' is the
## generic driver, whose message now names msil-ld. The stub's own texts are
## checked in stub.test; here they only show which driver ran: msil-ld names
## itself 'msil-ld' whatever the program name, while the GNU flavor names
## itself after the program and calls itself compatible with GNU linkers.
##
## The renamed programs are hard links to this build's lld in a fresh %t
## directory (Inputs/make-links.py), so a run costs no disk space. On Windows,
## lit runs a program through a lower-cased path, but LLVM there takes
## argv[0]'s file name from the module's file name on disk (GetModuleFileNameW
## and GetLongPathNameW, llvm/lib/Support/Windows/Process.inc), so the
## upper-case 'MSIL' component reaches the flavor dispatch (parseProgname) as
## written on every host. The GNU-flavor copy MSILx-ld shows this, because its
## 'no input files' error names the program; it gets a directory of its own,
## because on a case-insensitive file system it would be the same file as
## msilx-ld.

# DEFINE: %{fc} = FileCheck %s --implicit-check-not="PLEASE submit" \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not="Stack dump" --implicit-check-not="LLVM ERROR" \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not=error: --implicit-check-not=warning:

# RUN: msil-ld --version 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=VERSION
# RUN: %lld_exe -flavor msil --version 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=VERSION
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor msil 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=NOINPUT
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor msil %s 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=LINK

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir %t
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/make-links.py %lld_exe %t x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil-ld \
# RUN:   x86_64-w64-windows-gnu-msil-ld aarch64-pc-windows-msvc-msil-ld aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu-MSIL-ld \
# RUN:   msilx-ld x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-ld

# RUN: %t/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil-ld --version 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=VERSION
# RUN: not %t/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil-ld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=NOINPUT
# RUN: not %t/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil-ld %s 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=LINK
# RUN: %t/x86_64-w64-windows-gnu-msil-ld --version 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=VERSION
# RUN: not %t/x86_64-w64-windows-gnu-msil-ld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=NOINPUT
# RUN: not %t/x86_64-w64-windows-gnu-msil-ld %s 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=LINK
# RUN: not %t/aarch64-pc-windows-msvc-msil-ld %s 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=LINK
# RUN: not %t/aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu-MSIL-ld %s 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=LINK

## Not msil-ld.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/make-links.py %lld_exe %t/upper MSILx-ld
# RUN: not ld.lld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GNU -DNAME=ld.lld
# RUN: not %t/msilx-ld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GNU -DNAME=msilx-ld
# RUN: not %t/upper/MSILx-ld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GNU -DNAME=MSILx-ld
# RUN: not %t/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-ld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GNU -DNAME=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-ld
# RUN: %t/msilx-ld --version 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GNU-VERSION
# RUN: not %lld_exe 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GENERIC
# RUN: not %lld_exe --version 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=GENERIC

## The other flavors dispatch as before (their --version texts cannot show
## it, because lld-link, ld64.lld, wasm-ld and msil-ld print the same line).
## lld-link and 'lld -flavor link' reach the COFF flavor, which still misses
## only inputs when given /machine:x64, a COFF option that the GNU flavor and
## msil-ld read as an input file. ld64.lld and 'lld -flavor darwin' reach the
## Mach-O flavor, the only one that asks for -arch. wasm-ld and 'lld -flavor
## wasm' reach the wasm flavor, which still misses only inputs when given
## --no-entry, an option that the GNU flavor and msil-ld reject. -flavor maps
## only 'msil' to msil-ld (5.9.1), so 'ld-msil' is an unknown flavor. The
## -flavor forms name the program 'lld'.
# RUN: not lld-link 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=COFF -DNAME=lld-link
# RUN: not lld-link /machine:x64 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=COFF -DNAME=lld-link
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor link 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=COFF -DNAME=lld
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor link /machine:x64 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=COFF -DNAME=lld
# RUN: not ld64.lld 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=MACHO -DNAME=ld64.lld
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor darwin 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=MACHO -DNAME=lld
# RUN: not wasm-ld --no-entry 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=WASM -DNAME=wasm-ld
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor wasm --no-entry 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=WASM -DNAME=lld
# RUN: not %lld_exe -flavor ld-msil 2>&1 | %{fc} --check-prefix=BADFLAVOR

# VERSION:      {{^}}LLD {{.+}}

# NOINPUT:      {{^}}msil-ld: error: no input files{{$}}

# LINK:         {{^}}msil-ld: error: linking is not implemented yet{{$}}

# GNU:          {{^}}[[NAME]]: error: no input files{{$}}

# GNU-VERSION:  {{^}}LLD {{.+}} (compatible with GNU linkers){{$}}

# GENERIC:      {{^}}lld is a generic driver.{{$}}
# GENERIC-NEXT: {{^}}Invoke {{.+}}, msil-ld (MSIL) instead{{$}}

# COFF:         {{^}}[[NAME]]: error: no input files{{$}}

# MACHO:        {{^}}[[NAME]]: error: must specify -arch{{$}}

# WASM:         {{^}}[[NAME]]: error: no input files{{$}}

# BADFLAVOR:    {{^}}Unknown flavor: ld-msil{{$}}
